WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.437
NARRATOR: The moon is
a necessary first step

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.506
for exploration of the planets.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:10.009
To fly them there and return
them safely in this decade

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.679
is the goal of NASA's
project Apollo.

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:16.583
The early missions of Mercury
and the experience from Gemini

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:20.487
have brought this country to the
next major milestone, the first

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:23.022
Apollo 3-manned spaceflight.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.924
These are the men
to fly that mission,

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:28.628
command pilot Virgil Grissom,
Mercury, Gemini, and now

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:29.896
Apollo.

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:32.665
His third time into space,
one of the original seven

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:33.800
astronauts.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.702
The senior pilot
Edward White, he

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:39.339
will be remembered for his
spacewalk during Gemini 4.

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:41.374
White has been specializing
in the computers

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.743
and training for the
upcoming mission.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.046
Astronaut Roger Chaffee will
man the third Apollo seat.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:50.650
He has been concentrating on
the flight plan and experiments.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.792
ROGER CHAFFEE: I think
everybody in the space program

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.326
has been asked this 50 times.

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.061
And it's probably
the toughest question

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:07.801
to answer and not sound, shall
we say, corny with the answer.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.803
It's a new phase of exploration.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:17.644
It's-- you might say, and sound
a little trite, it's there.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.514
We'd be neglecting our duties as
people, as human beings, if we

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:24.651
didn't try to investigate it.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.220
We're improving our
engineering capability.

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:28.988
We're building
new equipment that

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:32.258
has untold number
of uses in fields

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.761
that we can't even
conceive of today.

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:37.597
The scientific aspect,
I don't think anybody

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:41.201
can predict what
it's going to be.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.469
Things that we'll find there.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.737
Some of the basic
geologic things

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:48.041
that we might find there that
have long since been destroyed

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:50.643
by weather on
Earth might give us

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.214
more insight into the
birth of our universe,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.851
the birth of our solar system.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.520
INTERVIEWER: You flew on--
on Mercury, flew on Gemini,

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:02.822
now you're flying on Apollo.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:06.526
Does the law of averages,
so far as the possibility

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:09.496
of a catastrophic failure,
bother you at all, sir?

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:10.864
VIRGIL GRISSOM: No,
you sort of have

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.333
to put that out of your mind.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:16.703
There's always a
possibility that you

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:19.305
can have a catastrophic
failure, of course.

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:20.640
It's going to happen
on any flight.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:21.708
It can happen on--

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.243
on the last one as
well as the first one.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:26.746
So you'll just plan,
as best you can,

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:31.251
to take care of all of
these eventualities.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:35.388
You get a well-trained
crew, and they go fly.

